\begin{abstract}
Generative models of discrete {3D} structure --- LEGO assemblies, voxel
buildings, block worlds --- are almost always evaluated by borrowing the image
pipeline: render the object from a few views, embed the renders with a
pretrained vision encoder, and report a Fr\'echet or kernel distance against
real data. We show that this pipeline has a specific and severe structural
blindness. We construct \solidify{}, a probe that fills every enclosed air
cell of a held-out corpus of real buildings with the build's own dominant
material: it adds a median of $31\%$ more blocks, deletes every room in $91\%$
of builds, and leaves the exterior silhouette untouched. A multi-view
DINOv2 kernel distance \emph{cannot separate the result from held-out real
data} ($0.000$ vs.\ a real floor of $-0.001$; the same paired, Holm-corrected
test at the same sample size does separate verbatim training copies at a
difference of $0.015$). This is not a power failure and not weak sensitivity:
across the full dose axis from $0\%$ to $100\%$ of interiors filled, the render
metric's Spearman correlation with the dose is $+0.300$, it is not monotone, and
its total dynamic range is \emph{zero} noise floors. A generator that emits
solid blobs shaped like houses is invisible to the metric the field reports.

We then build the evaluation that does see it. A geometric kernel distance over
D4-invariant voxel descriptors places the same probe $202$ real-sample spreads
from real data, runs on CPU, and passes a ladder of blocking validity gates that
Fr\'echet distance, coverage, density, precision and the biased MMD estimator
all fail. But it is blind in the complementary direction --- a material
permutation moves it by \emph{exactly} zero --- so neither tier is a
replacement for the other. We combine them into \blockscore{}, an aggregate
that takes the \emph{worst} pillar rather than the mean and treats novelty and
diversity as disqualification gates rather than terms, because a realism-only
leaderboard is won by reciting the training set (verbatim copies score second
best of sixteen arms). We also make explicit a compression that is known but unexploited: a
squared-MMD metric responds \emph{quadratically} to the fraction of an arm that
is bad, so a model failing on a quarter of its prompts scores under a tenth of
the way to fully bad. Reporting the square root instead recovers the failure
rate to a mean absolute error of $0.022$ against $0.119$.

Finally, we report a case study in which the benchmark overturned our own
headline result: a provenance gate revealed that our best-scoring generator had
$87\%$ of the test set in its training data, and retraining on a clean
group-aware split moved it from a significance group of its own into a tie with
the baselines it had appeared to beat. We release the suite, the probes, the
procedural baselines and the corpus split.
\end{abstract}
